THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
On a simple odd attack cycle (e.g., a -> b -> c -> a) the original grounded extension suspends every node because no node is unattacked and no status can be derived. The interval-lattice reformulation must assign each node a closed interval [lower, upper] of possible commitments, but on such a cycle the only consistent assignment is a degenerate interval where lower = upper, collapsing suspension back into a single point and removing the claimed semantic content of 'undecided' as a non-degenerate interval. Thus the interval semantics does not coincide with the grounded extension on the standard odd-cycle case: it either fails to produce a non-degenerate interval (contradicting the proposal's central claim) or it widens the interval in a way that no longer matches the skeptical fixed point, violating preservation of the existing semantics.

TARGET:
Reformulate the skeptical semantics on an interval lattice: each node's status is a closed interval [lower, upper] of possible commitments, and the grounded extension is the least fixed point of a monotone operator on this lattice. Suspension is then a non-degenerate interval rather than a third diagnostic label, so 'undecided' carries semantic content (the range of commitments consistent with the graph) and determinism follows from the Knaster–Tarski least-fixed-point theorem rather than from enumeration discipline. Criticizable: interval semantics may fail to coincide with grounded extension on graphs with odd attack cycles; widening/abstract-interpretation machinery adds complexity that may exceed polynomial bounds; interaction with attack-defeater warrants (SRC_002) and support chains (SRC_005) is uncharacterized.